- The Court must be guided by what, consistent with the law applicable to the minor, appears to be for the welfare of the minor — **Guardians and Wards Act 1890, s. 17(1)**.  
- In assessing the welfare of the minor, the Court shall have regard to the minor’s **age, sex and religion** — **s. 17(2)**.  
- Additionally, the Court may consider factors such as:  
  • The minor’s **near relations and their residence**;  
  • Whether a guardian has already been appointed by a person entitled to do so;  
  • Any prior applications made to any Court regarding the guardianship of the minor — **Guardians and Wards Act 1890, s. 10(f)‑(g)**.

